Frontline is made possible by contributions to your PBS station from viewers like you, thank you,

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.660
and by the Corporation for Public Broadcasting.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:23.360
Major support for Frontline is provided by the John D. and Catherine T. MacArthur Foundation,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.040
committed to building a more just, verdant, and peaceful world.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.970
More information is available at macfound.com.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.960
Additional funding is provided by the Park Foundation,

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.780
dedicated to heightening public awareness of critical issues.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:43.130
And by the Frontline Journalism Fund,

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:46.670
supporting investigative reporting and enterprise journalism.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.840
In late 2009, an army intel

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.140
An intelligence analyst, stationed in eastern Iraq,

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.510
logged on to a classified server.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.760
He opened a file that had been flagged by Army lawyers.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:51.200
It was footage taken from a U.S. Army helicopter gunship.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:23.880
Very soon after, Daniel Domscheit-Berg, who was working with the whistleblowing website

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.580
WikiLeaks, saw the video.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:37.040
There was this poor man crouching through the streets for his life.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.220
You get the feeling that these guys really want to shoot

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.240
and they ask for permission to shoot a couple of times.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.720
And then you get this short moment of hope

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.700
where the civilians arrive in a van

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.900
and you feel like, okay, finally at least.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:18.790
This guy has made it, and then they're all dying, and the whole van is blown up.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:24.610
Among the dead were two Reuters journalists and other civilians.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.960
Did you know what...

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:31.720
you wanted to do with it?

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.090
Publish. That was clear.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:40.630
My name is Julian Assange. I'm the editor of WikiLeaks.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.960
WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange unveiled the footage in a press conference in Washington.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:52.280
The material is dramatic. It was classified. It is still classified.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:57.920
There were a lot of people that were in favor of just publishing it.

00:04:58.610 --> 00:04:59.970
It seems that Julian had...

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.300
this crazy idea right in the beginning to sell it.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:06.400
There was a news agency that tried very hard to get hold of this.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:12.860
His idea was to market it for one million dollars exclusively to CNN or someone else

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:13.700
who would be interested.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:17.480
The material itself you see is significant and important.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:22.610
In the end, Assange edited the material into a short presentation with a provocative

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:24.670
title, Collateral Murder.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:29.880
You can identify the journalists immediately by the cameras they have.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:36.380
over the next year, the video would be seen on YouTube over 11 million times.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.770
Were you satisfied with the impact of the video?

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.860
Did it achieve the result that you had hoped?

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.590
It was pretty close.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:51.430
Could we have structured things, structured various deals,

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:55.070
economic incentives and so on, to get an even bigger impact?

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:57.030
The answer is probably yes.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:58.840
We can't discuss...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:11.010
sourcing of the video." Then, just seven weeks after Assange released his video,

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.020
Private in Iraq was arrested.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:18.380
... Army Private Bradley Manning was arrested outside of Baghdad and is now in a military

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:19.060
prison.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.540
He was a 22-year-old intelligence analyst who had been in country for just seven months.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:29.970
This is the story of Bradley Manning, WikiLeaks.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.960
and how more than half a million secret government documents came into public view.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.740
We begin a year before Manning was deployed to Iraq.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:53.720
He was stationed at a base in upstate New York.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:58.760
He posted these photos on Facebook and called it Fort Frostbite.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:05.150
Manning's Facebook page, to which Frontline obtained access,

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.160
tracks his life in the military.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.800
Manning had become an analyst at a time of increased intelligence sharing,

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:18.670
part of a series of post-9-11 reforms

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:21.620
meant to give analysts like Manning more data.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:28.150
Prior to 9-11, information was more compartmentalized or stovepiped.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.240
Stovepipe is a corridor, you know, where no one can get into it.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:38.120
There were CIA stovepipes, there were FBI stovepipes, there were DOD stovepipes.

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:47.030
So there was an effort made in the wake of 9-11 to integrate information, share it,

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:53.120
integrate it, and pass it around in real time with the proper use of our modern technologies.

00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:59.660
Manning was assigned to battalion headquarters.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:08.100
He did intelligence work for the unit's commanding officers in preparation for their deployment to Iraq.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:11.860
Manning liked his assignment.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.800
He talked about the fact that he was smart.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:19.950
It was able to get him in a different position, doing something different,

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:23.010
so that he wasn't down there with the guys that he called the grunts.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:29.920
He didn't tell me any specifics, but he did mention that he was consulted by.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.980
those who were much higher rank than he was.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:33.960
And it was a source of pride to him.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:38.120
But while he was proud to be entrusted with state secrets,

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:40.840
he chose not to keep his private life secret.

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:44.560
On Facebook, he joined groups like

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:46.520
Repeal Don't Ask, Don't Tell

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:48.630
and Liked Gay Marriage.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:53.530
It worried his father.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:59.340
I did see some comments I thought were pretty...

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.590
It was incriminating as far as don't ask, don't tell, you know, he was kind of asking

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:04.050
for trouble.

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:08.150
Brian Manning is a former Navy intelligence analyst.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:10.260
I thought that was pretty risky.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:16.900
Hey, you know, aren't you going against a policy that's in place where you're at?

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:21.760
Manning was taking a big risk.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:28.190
Under the Army's don't ask, don't tell rules, gay soldiers like Manning were required to

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:29.970
keep their sexual orientations...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.510
his friends also worried about his political activism.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:40.680
In his Facebook profile,

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.160
he posted signs and pictures at his presence at rallies.

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:45.540
Gay rights rallies. Right.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:53.100
This struck me as very dangerous to his position.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:57.910
I mean, I admired him for his courage on this,

00:09:58.030 --> 00:09:59.970
but I thought it might be a little bit foolhardy.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:08.790
During this period, Manning also started a relationship with a young man from upstate New York named Tyler Watkins.

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:14.090
On weekends, Bradley would visit him in Boston, where Watkins was studying.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:23.380
During those trips, the young intelligence analyst also found a new group of friends, computer science students and hackers.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:29.290
At the time, WikiLeaks was already making headlines.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:33.210
And Julian Assange was an admired figure among hackers.

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:41.040
Boston opened new doors for Manning, but he had a problem back on base.

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:48.530
He thought that this one officer dude had it out for him,

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:52.220
that he didn't like him, and that he thought that a lot of the things they did

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:56.380
were inefficient and that, you know, there were better ways of doing things.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:59.000
Manning had a history of trouble.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.350
Before he joined the Army, he had struggled to keep a steady job.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.760
It was his father who pushed him to sign up.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:11.440
He enlisted at 19.

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:17.880
Five foot two, Manning was the smallest guy in his unit.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:22.890
Boot camp didn't go well.

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:25.760
You could tell the Army was not for Manning.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:29.980
The drill sergeant did not like him.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.540
at all. And they would just pick on him constantly. You know, that was not a good push-up. Go do 20

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:44.020
more. Since he was gay and effeminate and small and tiny, he could do a small little thing and

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:49.750
they would just try to make his life a living hell. Despite being harassed, Manning fought back.

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:55.320
He tried to fight and argue because Manning is a very vocal person. Even though he's picked

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:59.980
out a lot, he would speak for himself. He would yell back and fight.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.340
And that's, I think, what caused a lot of drill sergeants to become even more.

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:13.280
It was a vicious circle.

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:15.660
Manning started getting into fights.

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:21.420
At Fort Drum, he was reprimanded for tossing chairs and yelling at fellow soldiers.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:23.830
He was referred for counseling.

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:29.970
But because of Don't Ask, Don't Tell, Manning was unable to...

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:31.550
confide in his army therapist.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.760
He sought counseling off-base.

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:38.290
He called me from Fort Drum

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:42.110
after some clashes between him and officers

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:44.020
or other enlisted men,

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:48.540
and mostly it was just him crying over the phone to me.

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:49.380
Crying? Sobbing?

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:51.020
Very violently.

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:52.360
And what would he be saying?

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:56.330
A lot of it was intelligible.

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:59.970
Mostly it was why he claimed that his superiors were...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.750
stupid, and I can't stay in this situation. I'm never going to get out."

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:14.910
His army supervisor was concerned about sending Manning to Iraq, worried that he was a risk

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:23.480
both to himself and others. But there was a shortage of qualified analysts. He was sent

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:23.940
anyway.

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:29.980
During the time that he's either in training or...

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.640
After his deployment overseas, you had no communications with him?

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:36.160
You knew nothing about any troubles that he might be having?

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:40.590
Not an email, not a text message, not a letter, nothing.

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:43.420
Did you write to him?

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:43.950
No.

00:13:44.260 --> 00:13:45.660
I don't even know his address.

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:58.210
Manning arrived at Forward Operating Base Hammer in mid-October 2009.

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.410
with a handful of intelligence analysts

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:05.420
in a top-secret facility called a SCIF.

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:08.560
Well, a SCIF is simply a facility

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:11.720
that meets very specific, very rigid standards

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:14.260
to safeguard the classified information that's stored inside.

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:17.340
Anybody who has access, physical access,

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:23.320
to those networks has been given a great deal of trust

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:24.440
by the United States government.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:29.520
The two classified networks Manning had access to were JWICs,

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.120
which contains high-level military secrets,

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:35.620
and Cipranet, which stores hundreds of thousands

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:37.060
of diplomatic cables.

00:14:38.950 --> 00:14:41.190
There's just been a theory in the military,

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:42.990
over the last decade especially,

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:45.510
that the lower you push this information,

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:47.310
the more you empower your people,

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:51.050
the greater strategic benefit you're going to ultimately see.

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:53.260
You know, the platoon and squad level

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:55.700
can have an order of magnitude

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:59.620
of transformational impact during the war.

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:05.730
There was a flood of information that analysts, like Manning, would have had access to.

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:10.880
Every helicopter and every drone records its missions.

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:20.370
Soldiers wear helmet cams.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:28.740
And every day, soldiers in the field file detailed reports of their battles.

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:33.800
their encounters with informants, and more.

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:37.080
These reports are the war logs.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:39.720
We're talking about thousands of people.

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:43.170
In really every military command that there is,

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:45.210
there's an office, there's computers.

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:47.950
We have information that allow analysts to access

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:51.350
just an absolute cornucopia of material.

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:55.380
Security controls on this information can vary.

00:15:57.680 --> 00:15:59.960
Manning could bring recordable CDs into...

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.220
his skiff apparently without problems.

00:16:04.450 --> 00:16:08.750
That he was playing Lady Gaga or whatever on a classified workstation in a war zone,

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:10.050
I can't speak to that.

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:15.280
Obviously you're not allowed to plug in any unclassified media, and if you do, that media

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:18.280
becomes classified at the level of the workstation immediately.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:26.550
And there was nothing preventing Manning from downloading data, and no software in place

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:29.150
to detect unusual downloading activity.

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:45.040
The idea that this guy could download confidential and classified information onto a CD or plug in a memory stick into a computer,

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:51.120
and A, it would allow you to do that, and B, there was no red flag that went up for a supervisor to say,

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:55.280
you know, why is this guy spending so much time doing this kind of thing?

00:16:55.420 --> 00:16:56.460
I mean, that was shocking.

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:05.380
In January 2010, Manning would return to the U.S. on leave.

00:17:06.340 --> 00:17:13.880
He went to see Tyler Watkins, but their relationship was on the rocks.

00:17:14.740 --> 00:17:19.210
According to his friend, Jason Edwards, Manning was feeling abandoned.

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:24.390
I asked him about this. I said, do you have anyone that you can confide in?

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:27.890
Do you have anyone that you really would consider your friend?

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:29.610
And he assured me that he had nothing.

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:37.850
And so for him, Tyler was, Tyler was where he put all the,

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:40.350
that was the one basket in which he put all his emotional eggs.

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:45.590
It was during that week in Boston that Manning attended this party

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:49.900
at Boston University's hackerspace, hosted by David House.

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:54.460
I was a senior at Boston University in computer science,

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:59.970
and I founded this makerspace at BU called BUILDS, BU Information Lab.

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:05.280
design space. Bradley showed up to the open house of this event. This was a night where

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:10.960
the room was absolutely packed. He didn't strike me at all at that time as being a particularly

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:16.040
memorable, remarkable individual. What he was doing was mostly socializing, meeting others,

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:23.550
trying to learn about how this scene in Boston actually functioned. Standing back, leaning

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:26.140
against the table, is Bradley Manning.

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:37.870
The young intelligence analyst, full of secrets, was mingling among hackers.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:45.570
Investigators now believe that sometime in this period,

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:49.500
Manning either uploaded or handed off two large data files,

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:53.170
the war logs, from Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:18:55.170 --> 00:18:59.910
We don't really know whether Manning approached WikiLeaks or people around.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.340
WikiLeaks or it was the other way around. But my theory is, whichever way it is, there's an

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:09.260
intermediary. There's a group of people in the middle, probably these people in Cambridge,

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:16.300
Massachusetts, who are kind of former computer hackers, many of whom are supporters, are kind

00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:22.780
of in this loose network of people who support WikiLeaks. So somewhere in this mix, you have

00:19:22.780 --> 00:19:28.920
Manning with access to this information. You've got WikiLeaks and Julian Assange with the desire

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:29.980
to get it and you've got to

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.870
a helpful intermediary, and somewhere in between here,

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:34.770
there's a transfer, I believe, takes place.

00:19:35.050 --> 00:19:40.190
The question of how Assange acquired the documents is important.

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:44.620
Was Assange a passive recipient, or was he more involved?

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:48.180
I think Assange is savvy enough that he would have tried

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:53.640
to avoid at all costs any direct contact with Bradley Manning,

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:58.480
understanding that that could later lead to a much easier prosecution

00:19:58.480 --> 00:19:59.760
on the grounds of conspiracy.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:06.400
commit espionage. In other words, if WikiLeaks actively helped someone violate secrecy laws,

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:13.540
Assange and his colleagues could be held criminally liable. We had one rule at the core

00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:19.380
of this whole project, and that was not to solicit anything. This is a very important part of this

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:24.110
whole project. It's one of these golden rules that you need to have, is you do not solicit

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:29.770
material, you're just a conduit. We accept these documents anonymously. We have very strong

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:29.910
make

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:31.960
to protect the sources that we have.

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:35.520
Instead of keeping source identities secret,

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:37.360
we simply do not collect them at all.

00:20:37.480 --> 00:20:40.560
We've never lost a source and we've never lost a legal case.

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:43.400
We have technological means to protect people.

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:45.940
Assange says he designed WikiLeaks

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:48.660
so that he wouldn't even know who his sources were.

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:51.100
That sort of dedication to protecting sources

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:52.680
is something that our sources have seen.

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:55.740
We do not know whether Mr. Manning is our source or not.

00:20:57.200 --> 00:20:59.970
And of course, if we did know, we are.

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:03.680
obligated ethically to not reveal it.

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:06.820
But how do you prevent me from writing you and telling you in a chat

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:13.380
that I have a video of a massacre and I want you to tell me how to get it to you?

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:16.270
Our help desk has a completely anonymous chat.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:20.620
It's anonymous to us, the usernames are anonymous and so on.

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:29.460
In February 2010, Manning was back in Iraq.

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:37.990
And within a month or two, he allegedly loaded special data mining software onto his classified workstation

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:45.090
and started downloading more documents, including a quarter million confidential State Department cables.

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:52.980
Confidential information like, what did Secretary of Defense Gates say to his counterpart in Paris?

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:56.870
What did the ambassador in Paris say to Secretary Gates?

00:21:57.620 --> 00:21:58.660
But it's confidential.

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:03.740
Bradley Manning, for example, does not need to know

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:06.540
what the Secretary of Defense said to his counterpart in Paris.

00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:14.100
By late April, Bradley Manning's private world was falling apart.

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:18.320
When Manning saw on Facebook that his friend Tyler Watkins

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:21.120
was in a new relationship, he lashed out.

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:25.520
If you don't start answering some goddamn questions, he wrote,

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:27.520
there will be a hell of a scene.

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:29.960
I really thought he was going to...

00:22:30.680 --> 00:22:33.660
I just was waiting for the news that he was going to blow his head off.

00:22:35.420 --> 00:22:38.730
According to an Army report, a week after the breakup,

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:40.790
Manning was found in a fetal position.

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:46.280
He had a knife and had etched on a chair the words, I want.

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:54.980
The last message I remember seeing from him was something to the effect

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:57.690
that there was news that was going to shock the world,

00:22:58.640 --> 00:22:59.680
and that I would hear about it.

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:16.230
In late May, a hacker in California was contacted

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:21.300
and began a chat with someone using the screen name Bradass87.

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:25.740
If you had free reign over classified networks

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:29.200
and you saw incredible things, awful things,

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:29.960
what would you do?

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:30.600
What would you do?

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:35.220
He spoke in hypotheticals at first.

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:40.370
Even then, I largely blew it off.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:47.100
There just aren't enough hours in the day to correspond with everybody that wants to

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:48.410
correspond with me.

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:53.320
The hacker, Adrian Lama, was well-known in the cyber underground.

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:58.140
In 2003, he'd been arrested for hacking the New York Times.

00:23:58.140 --> 00:23:59.970
and the day begins.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:04.190
Before the chat, Lamo was featured in an article on Wired.com

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:06.770
that discussed his psychiatric problems.

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:12.880
Brad S87 would keep up his chat with Lamo for the next four days.

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:18.020
He talked a lot about his personal life, about his relationships,

00:24:18.610 --> 00:24:22.990
about his experiences in the military, about experiences back home.

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:29.970
Lamo says the person also contacted him via email, and they became friends.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:38.880
on Facebook. It was Bradley Manning, intelligence analyst in Iraq. And in his profile, there was

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:48.660
the chat name, Bradass87. He read more. Looking at his Facebook page, I got the sense that Bradley

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:57.380
was very depressed. Then during one of their chats, Bradass87 started dropping hints about

00:24:57.380 --> 00:24:58.960
a crazy white-haired Aussie.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.600
He mentioned Julian Assange in the context Julian was the individual at WikiLeaks

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:07.760
who he had initially established contact with.

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:12.780
He also mentioned that he had leaked thousands of classified documents,

00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:16.120
including a huge tranche of diplomatic cables.

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:21.040
I asked him if there was any way to recover the documents.

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:26.140
He indicated that they had already been uploaded to WikiLeaks' server.

00:25:27.320 --> 00:25:29.810
It was fairly unambiguous.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:36.640
statement. Lamo says he believed Manning was a security risk and worried that if he didn't say

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:43.400
something, he could be party to a crime. He called an old friend, Tim Webster. Adrian was

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:48.960
quite conflicted. We had a back and forth where he was concerned about whether or not this was the

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:55.890
right thing to do, whether to betray this person's trust was appropriate given his actions. Adrian

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:59.980
recognized that we can't have somebody out there leaking classified information.

00:26:00.940 --> 00:26:03.170
It's not something he can allow to continue.

00:26:04.020 --> 00:26:07.140
Webster coached Lamo to keep up the chat as long as possible

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:08.800
to find out more details.

00:26:09.890 --> 00:26:11.170
He also alerted the Pentagon

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:13.260
that they may have a security breach.

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:16.540
Lamo was nervous and felt trapped.

00:26:18.210 --> 00:26:20.690
There was no correct option.

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:23.110
There was only the least incorrect one.

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:26.850
Either way, I would have been squaring somebody over.

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:29.980
I had to pick who. There was no...

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:36.320
option to just sit back and wash my hands of the responsibility because that in and of itself would

00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:44.290
have been making a choice. Lamo then called a reporter he knew at Wired.com. He called me to

00:26:44.290 --> 00:26:50.920
tell me that he had a meeting set up the next day with the FBI and the army because he was turning

00:26:50.920 --> 00:26:57.460
in somebody who'd contacted him online and confessed to passing classified information

00:26:57.460 --> 00:26:59.780
to someone he described as a foreign national.

00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:04.040
Poulsen convinced Lamo to hand him a copy of the chat

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:07.280
and then asked a colleague to follow up on any leads.

00:27:07.940 --> 00:27:11.260
We went through the chat logs and we looked at what else he mentioned.

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:13.620
Tyler Watkins was named in the chat logs.

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:16.680
Zetter called Watkins.

00:27:17.330 --> 00:27:20.190
He told her about a conversation he'd had with Manning

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:22.800
during Manning's January visit to Boston.

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:28.440
Brad had told him that he had uncovered information that concerned him

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:29.840
and he was considering leaking it.

00:27:32.240 --> 00:27:37.300
And so he was weighing that, whether or not the good that he felt he would be doing

00:27:37.300 --> 00:27:41.950
in leaking the information outweighed any kind of, you know,

00:27:42.050 --> 00:27:44.530
personal suffering that he might undergo for leaking it.

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:53.010
On May 26, 2010, Bradley Manning was arrested.

00:27:55.290 --> 00:27:56.550
It was posted on Facebook.

00:27:58.840 --> 00:27:59.980
We got confirmation that...

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:03.580
Manning had been arrested when Manning called his aunt from jail.

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:07.600
And asked her to update his Facebook page with a message.

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:12.460
It would be his final Facebook entry.

00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:17.040
I just couldn't, I couldn't believe it.

00:28:17.260 --> 00:28:18.240
It didn't seem true.

00:28:19.500 --> 00:28:21.600
I guess part of me probably believed that this would,

00:28:23.070 --> 00:28:25.620
that this would turn out to not, you know, be anything.

00:28:28.500 --> 00:28:29.960
Well, I guess I was wrong.

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:43.920
Please welcome Julian Assange.

00:28:44.510 --> 00:28:48.300
In the weeks before Manning was arrested,

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:52.010
Julian Assange was on the road promoting WikiLeaks.

00:28:52.340 --> 00:28:57.700
So I'm not sure how many people here are familiar with the basics of my work.

00:28:57.860 --> 00:28:59.130
And I'll just try and go very briefly...

00:28:59.460 --> 00:28:59.980
Since 2000...

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:04.470
In 2006, he had already amassed an impressive record of leaking secrets.

00:29:06.670 --> 00:29:10.770
But the collateral murder video had taken WikiLeaks to a new level.

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:15.330
One classified video can possibly stop a war.

00:29:16.570 --> 00:29:20.260
The news of Manning's arrest hit WikiLeaks like a bomb.

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:29.970
It was terrible. That one problem occurred that we had always feared, which is what...

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.800
one of our potential sources would be exposed.

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:35.400
We had this picture all over the Internet

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:39.020
of this really sympathetic-looking guy.

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:41.280
And he's such a young person,

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:43.490
so it's kind of extraordinarily

00:29:43.490 --> 00:29:45.930
that someone at this age

00:29:45.930 --> 00:29:49.020
has these really profound views on things.

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:54.140
The outing of an alleged source was bad enough,

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:58.090
but Wired.com had also published portions of the Lamo chat,

00:29:58.520 --> 00:29:59.960
complete with Manning's references.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:03.780
to Assange, WikiLeaks, and to documents he had leaked.

00:30:05.050 --> 00:30:08.660
Assange was anxious and frightened initially when our story came out.

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:12.440
In fact, he contacted me, and he wanted the chat logs,

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:16.920
and he said that he needed it in order to prepare Manning's defense.

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:21.230
But, you know, it was to prepare Manning's defense for who?

00:30:22.630 --> 00:30:25.330
You know, I can only speculate, but I think that he was concerned

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:27.390
about what was in the chat logs about himself.

00:30:28.650 --> 00:30:29.970
Zetter refused to hand the...

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:37.690
over. Assange then tried getting the entire chat from Lamo. Assange sent me an email after Bradley

00:30:37.690 --> 00:30:45.210
was arrested encouraging me to change my characterization of the events, to refer to

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:54.100
Manning as a whistleblower rather than a spy, and ascribe my motivations to a momentary lapse of

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:59.860
reason. Adrian Lamo, you wrote him an email after the fact. Yes, that's correct.

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:04.770
and you encouraged him to change his characterisation of the events.

00:31:05.780 --> 00:31:09.680
Yes, I thought that if there was a chance that...

00:31:10.020 --> 00:31:12.380
Regardless, let me clarify.

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:17.630
We are in a very difficult position concerning Bradley Manning.

00:31:19.990 --> 00:31:24.630
The difficulty of our position is that our technology does not permit us to understand

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:28.180
whether someone is one of our sources or not,

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:29.980
because the best way to keep a secret...

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:30.840
is to never have it.

00:31:32.740 --> 00:31:38.820
But in his chat with Lamo, Brad S87 says he did not use the normal WikiLeaks submission system.

00:31:39.790 --> 00:31:42.720
He says he had a relationship with Assange.

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:47.740
He stated that he had spent four months trying to track Assange down

00:31:47.740 --> 00:31:53.300
and reassure himself of the identity of the person that he was communicating with.

00:31:53.420 --> 00:31:56.440
That is, to make sure that it actually was Assange.

00:31:57.010 --> 00:31:59.970
People who've read the chat raise several...

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:05.730
instances where he talks about you and his relationship, and when you read it, it sounds

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:10.670
like there is a connection, perhaps a chat or email or some kind of connection with you.

00:32:10.960 --> 00:32:20.220
We looked at the whole context. Was this someone trying to big note themselves by suggesting

00:32:20.220 --> 00:32:26.610
a connection to us? We don't have sources that we know about. I'd never heard the name

00:32:26.610 --> 00:32:29.970
Bradley Manning before. I'd never heard the name Bradass87 before.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:40.310
At a major conference last year, Assange was also asked if Manning was his source for the video and the cables.

00:32:40.630 --> 00:32:44.970
There's been this U.S. intelligence analyst, Bradley Manning, arrested,

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:50.090
and it's alleged that he confessed in a chat room to have leaked this video to you,

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:56.600
along with 280,000 classified U.S. embassy cables. I mean, did he?

00:32:57.750 --> 00:32:59.970
We have denied receiving those cables.

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:05.840
Assange was facing a dilemma.

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:09.270
If WikiLeaks acknowledged having more documents

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:10.850
alleged to have come from Manning,

00:33:11.370 --> 00:33:13.210
he risked further harm to his source.

00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:17.660
Did you discuss internally amongst yourselves

00:33:17.660 --> 00:33:20.060
whether or not releasing the war logs

00:33:20.060 --> 00:33:23.700
and eventually the cables could further jeopardize him?

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:27.990
There was discussion about, you know,

00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:29.530
we have a situation where there's...

00:33:30.090 --> 00:33:33.010
a young man held in a military prison under investigation,

00:33:34.860 --> 00:33:37.740
who's alleged to be a source for collateral murder video.

00:33:39.010 --> 00:33:43.870
But we have published and received military documents

00:33:43.870 --> 00:33:48.920
long before Bradley Manning ever joined the army.

00:33:49.020 --> 00:33:53.840
But he mentioned some of these packages allegedly in the chat.

00:33:54.260 --> 00:33:56.490
For example, in that alleged chat,

00:33:56.660 --> 00:33:59.800
he does not mention anything about the Afghan material.

00:34:00.160 --> 00:34:02.360
But the Iraq war logs and the cables are mentioned.

00:34:02.750 --> 00:34:04.910
He mentions a number of things.

00:34:05.510 --> 00:34:10.020
What we do know is that we promised the source

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:13.750
that we would publish everything that they gave to us.

00:34:13.870 --> 00:34:18.300
It was clear for me that these diplomatic cables should not be released.

00:34:19.090 --> 00:34:19.410
Why?

00:34:20.050 --> 00:34:22.650
Because it was unclear how much that would implicate

00:34:22.830 --> 00:34:24.570
someone that had gotten into trouble.

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:29.680
And I think it is, I mean, it's a very hard distinction.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:37.900
you have to take. What is more important, bringing the truth to the light or protecting

00:34:37.950 --> 00:34:39.610
one person?

00:34:39.830 --> 00:34:41.090
What is the right choice?

00:34:41.350 --> 00:34:45.790
I don't know. My gut and my heart say that you should protect the person.

00:34:50.640 --> 00:34:55.760
After Manning's arrest, Assange was scheduled to speak in the U.S. at a hackers conference.

00:34:56.590 --> 00:34:59.910
The person who leaked the video in the first place to

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:02.480
WikiLeaks should be considered a national hero.

00:35:06.470 --> 00:35:09.470
Talk of Assange and Manning dominated the discussions.

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:13.660
To set the record clear, I'm not an informant.

00:35:13.840 --> 00:35:15.680
I'm a witness in a criminal case.

00:35:16.160 --> 00:35:20.140
Adrian Lama sat on what was referred to as the snitch panel.

00:35:20.680 --> 00:35:26.420
Bradley Manning, the alleged leaker, is currently sitting in prison in Kuwait, I believe,

00:35:26.660 --> 00:35:29.780
and he could be locked up for the rest of his life.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:30.680
How do you feel about that?

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:39.480
I think that it's a little bit ludicrous to say that Bradley Manning is going to be

00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:39.860
tortured.

00:35:40.420 --> 00:35:43.760
We don't do that to our citizens.

00:35:47.950 --> 00:35:49.660
He got booed at many points.

00:35:50.150 --> 00:35:53.830
What he had done was probably the most unpopular thing ever in the hacker world.

00:35:54.770 --> 00:35:59.650
When someone alleged to have been responsible for bringing out the truth is...

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:06.560
imprisoned because of the actions of somebody else, their reaction to that is simply swift

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:12.710
and condemning. The crowd eagerly awaited their keynote speaker. How many of you expect to see

00:36:12.710 --> 00:36:19.130
Julian walk through that side door right now and come up onto this stage? Yeah? Conference

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:23.850
organizers believed there were half a dozen federal agents sitting in the audience. And how

00:36:23.850 --> 00:36:29.840
many of you live in the real world? Assange sent a surrogate. Hello.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:31.680
to all my friends and fans in domestic

00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:33.080
and international surveillance.

00:36:35.540 --> 00:36:38.040
I'm here today because I believe

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:39.380
that we can make a better world.

00:36:40.050 --> 00:36:42.480
Julian, unfortunately, can't make it

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:44.240
because we don't live in that better world.

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:47.440
It was rumored that documents were yet to be released.

00:36:48.040 --> 00:36:49.400
Having that kind of a rumor,

00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:51.500
that you have sensitive documents

00:36:51.970 --> 00:36:53.340
that could embarrass the U.S.

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:55.040
or cause national security issues,

00:36:55.220 --> 00:36:57.280
makes it a little difficult to travel to the country

00:36:57.280 --> 00:36:58.920
that's accusing you of that in the first place.

00:37:04.600 --> 00:37:06.480
For a month, Assange had been in hiding.

00:37:08.960 --> 00:37:10.000
Then he resurfaced.

00:37:11.470 --> 00:37:15.390
He had decided to go ahead and publish the Afghan and Iraq war logs.

00:37:15.760 --> 00:37:18.080
In all, nearly 500,000 files.

00:37:18.880 --> 00:37:19.920
He sought partners.

00:37:21.210 --> 00:37:25.620
He wanted to build this coalition, so that was his idea of how to go about this.

00:37:25.720 --> 00:37:29.060
Let's start working on a coalition between a couple of newspapers.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:31.940
The New York Times, and The Spiegel.

00:37:32.900 --> 00:37:34.660
We insisted on bringing in The New York Times.

00:37:35.790 --> 00:37:39.480
We also insisted on The New York Times publishing first.

00:37:41.340 --> 00:37:45.230
So if there was any debate before a jury about

00:37:45.230 --> 00:37:49.030
had it been published first in a foreign publication

00:37:49.030 --> 00:37:51.850
or a US publication, it would be very clear

00:37:51.850 --> 00:37:54.900
it was published first in a US publication.

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:56.160
Protected by the First Amendment.

00:37:56.200 --> 00:37:57.160
Protected by the First Amendment.

00:37:57.340 --> 00:37:59.970
Not an act of espionage by...

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:05.690
Exactly. Not an act of espionage. Clearly, that wasn't the case, but we didn't want anyone to try and spin it either.

00:38:07.270 --> 00:38:10.490
While Assange started working with The Guardian's reporters in London,

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:14.480
The Guardian's chief editor called his counterpart at The New York Times.

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:22.870
He bit by bit laid out the situation, which was that they had been given this enormous trove of information,

00:38:23.280 --> 00:38:28.700
hundreds of thousands of documents, dispatches from the field in Afghanistan and Iraq.

00:38:28.700 --> 00:38:29.970
There was, at that point,

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:33.120
an intimation that there might be more things to come,

00:38:33.160 --> 00:38:34.320
and was I interested?

00:38:34.780 --> 00:38:36.300
And clearly I was interested.

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:40.200
Over the next four weeks,

00:38:40.340 --> 00:38:42.820
The Times, Der Spiegel, and Guardian reporters

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:45.360
poured through the war logs looking for news.

00:38:46.190 --> 00:38:48.170
There was no one startling headline,

00:38:48.780 --> 00:38:50.500
but there was lots of rich detail.

00:38:51.580 --> 00:38:53.380
It was a remarkable insight.

00:38:53.580 --> 00:38:55.900
I mean, it was an unvarnished, rich portrait

00:38:55.900 --> 00:38:58.520
of the daily conduct of two wars.

00:38:59.090 --> 00:38:59.970
I would argue that...

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:02.360
She came away with stuff you didn't get in the Pentagon Papers

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:06.060
because of the rawness of the information,

00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:09.700
the sheer day-to-day mundane life of war.

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:14.480
In their coverage, the papers decided that they would black out the names

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:17.500
of any civilian informants working for the U.S. military.

00:39:19.120 --> 00:39:22.200
But Assange had a different idea for his WikiLeaks website.

00:39:22.930 --> 00:39:27.320
One evening, just days before publication, they confronted him over dinner.

00:39:28.120 --> 00:39:29.960
Julian, whose project was to...

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:37.030
the entire data set was very reluctant to delete those names, to redact them.

00:39:37.750 --> 00:39:41.630
We said, Julian, we've got to do something about these redactions, we really have got to.

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:48.160
And he said, these people were collaborators, informants, they deserve to die.

00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:51.780
And there was a sort of silence fell around the table.

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:56.020
The other journalists who were involved from all three news organisations

00:39:56.020 --> 00:39:58.700
were also raising the issue with him and getting the same answer.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:05.320
Part of what was steering his judgment was his origins as a hacker, a computer hacker,

00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:11.490
where there's a very purest ideology that all information should be accessible to everybody.

00:40:12.520 --> 00:40:15.620
Only at the eleventh hour would Assange change his mind.

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:18.000
His team was caught off guard.

00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:24.050
Four days before publication, 90,000 documents needed to be redacted.

00:40:24.900 --> 00:40:26.460
I mean, what do you do?

00:40:26.600 --> 00:40:29.400
It's 90,000 documents, and there's just no way.

00:40:30.090 --> 00:40:33.640
that anyone could screen 90,000 documents over the weekend.

00:40:35.670 --> 00:40:38.630
In a process Assange called harm minimization,

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:43.110
he agreed to hold back around 14,000 of the most sensitive documents.

00:40:44.240 --> 00:40:46.220
But it didn't protect everyone at risk.

00:40:47.270 --> 00:40:49.850
Within 48 hours of us publishing the Warlogs,

00:40:50.090 --> 00:40:52.250
hostile newspapers in New York and London

00:40:52.250 --> 00:40:54.550
who compete with The Guardian in The New York Times

00:40:54.550 --> 00:40:56.270
ran big stories saying,

00:40:56.270 --> 00:40:57.810
we've been on the WikiLeaks website,

00:40:58.030 --> 00:40:59.980
we found material which could get...

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:05.120
killed and that had a very damaging political impact on the way that the

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:09.560
story played out and also within WikiLeaks where Julian's colleagues were

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:14.970
horrified that their website was carrying this material and very angry that it was

00:41:14.970 --> 00:41:19.850
carrying that material and they'd never been told. The question of harm

00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:25.970
minimization came in for a lot of criticism of that, that you were in your

00:41:25.970 --> 00:41:29.060
initial conversations, not concerned.

00:41:30.090 --> 00:41:33.450
Absolutely false. And this is a typical rhetorical trick.

00:41:33.690 --> 00:41:35.230
Why does this keep coming up?

00:41:35.350 --> 00:41:39.430
Why are there people out there that are saying that you didn't care if informants were killed?

00:41:39.570 --> 00:41:40.390
It's absolutely false.

00:41:40.570 --> 00:41:49.370
But you reject the idea or the allegation that you ever resisted, that you were into just releasing the names.

00:41:49.900 --> 00:41:53.330
It's completely false. We have a harm minimization procedure.

00:41:53.630 --> 00:41:59.920
A harm minimization procedure is that we don't want innocent people who have a decent chance.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:01.660
of being hurt to be hurt.

00:42:01.840 --> 00:42:06.220
By the time the Iraq war logs were released,

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:07.970
they were heavily redacted.

00:42:09.320 --> 00:42:11.640
But the Times had lost trust in the process.

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:14.760
They refused to link to WikiLeaks

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:16.020
in any of their coverage.

00:42:18.190 --> 00:42:19.310
Assange was furious.

00:42:20.940 --> 00:42:22.660
Julian called me up and said that he took that

00:42:22.660 --> 00:42:23.760
as a sign of disrespect.

00:42:24.890 --> 00:42:26.510
It made him angry.

00:42:26.760 --> 00:42:27.520
What did you tell him?

00:42:28.060 --> 00:42:29.980
I said, we believe that in those...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:33.840
documents you posted are the names of innocent people whose lives could be put at risk.

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:41.500
We were not going to link to that. What did he say? He said, where's the respect? Assange threatened

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:48.100
to cut the Times out of any future deals, notably access to the 251,000 diplomatic cables.

00:42:49.540 --> 00:42:55.480
Then the Times published a profile of Bradley Manning. Julian was quite angry at the New York

00:42:55.480 --> 00:42:59.980
Times. Following on the heels of our disagreement about the linking, we wrote a...

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:05.780
about bradley manning which he very much didn't like assange's narrative was that bradley manning

00:43:05.780 --> 00:43:12.220
was a hero and that he did this for heroic purposes we wrote a much more textured story

00:43:12.220 --> 00:43:17.240
that also said he was a troubled guy who had a troubled relationship with the military assange

00:43:17.240 --> 00:43:23.100
hated that story he hated things we wrote about him he hated the fact that we were publicly critical

00:43:23.100 --> 00:43:29.400
of him so he didn't want us anywhere near these documents he gave them to the guardian he decided

00:43:29.400 --> 00:43:29.980
that he was going.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:34.780
to double-cross the New York Times and cut them out of the deal because he was angry.

00:43:35.020 --> 00:43:37.440
They had written a disobliging profile of him,

00:43:37.700 --> 00:43:42.940
describing him as imperious and crazy and, you know, impossible.

00:43:43.640 --> 00:43:46.800
We weren't willing to double-cross the New York Times.

00:43:46.900 --> 00:43:48.200
We thought a deal is a deal.

00:43:49.520 --> 00:43:52.320
The Guardian shared a copy of the cables with the Times.

00:43:53.560 --> 00:43:56.950
So on the evening of November 28, 2010,

00:43:57.740 --> 00:43:59.980
the New York Times, The Guardian, and...

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:18.350
published. It touched every country. It was the biggest story on the planet. Of all the

00:44:18.350 --> 00:44:22.030
files Manning is alleged to have leaked, this was the motherland.

00:44:23.370 --> 00:44:28.370
Whoever leaked that information is guilty of treason. And I think anything less than

00:44:28.370 --> 00:44:29.350
And execution is too common.

00:44:33.360 --> 00:44:40.270
Do you believe that actually capturing Mr. Assange and prosecuting him would send the right kind of signal?

00:44:41.030 --> 00:44:48.150
Mr. Assange has disclosed this material without regard to the risk that it does generate.

00:44:48.330 --> 00:44:50.230
Is there anything good to come out of this in your view?

00:44:50.830 --> 00:44:51.050
No.

00:44:52.010 --> 00:44:52.290
Nothing?

00:44:53.110 --> 00:44:59.920
The unauthorized release of 251,000 cables that covers every relationship the United States has.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:02.620
with countries around the world has done damage

00:45:02.620 --> 00:45:04.580
to the national interests of the United States.

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:08.650
This is a nightmare for U.S. diplomats.

00:45:08.810 --> 00:45:11.530
It was a window never before opened to the public.

00:45:11.570 --> 00:45:14.890
They are highly sensitive documents, never meant to be read by the public.

00:45:14.890 --> 00:45:18.310
Daily dispatches between 270 diplomatic outposts

00:45:18.310 --> 00:45:20.170
around the world and Washington.

00:45:20.450 --> 00:45:22.370
The Obama administration is in the midst

00:45:22.370 --> 00:45:24.690
of international damage control.

00:45:24.970 --> 00:45:28.480
The release exposed candid, often embarrassing, assessments.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:36.470
certainly been a pretty serious irritant in a number of places. And to the extent that

00:45:36.470 --> 00:45:42.170
cooperating with other countries, multilateralism, partnerships around the world, to the extent

00:45:42.170 --> 00:45:49.510
that these kind of leaks undermine friendships, they're harmful. Is it a nuclear bomb? No,

00:45:49.900 --> 00:45:57.400
but it's pretty serious. According to U.S. diplomats, it's made it harder for them to do

00:45:57.400 --> 00:45:58.900
their job. Was that the intent?

00:46:00.060 --> 00:46:03.320
if they are embarrassed by what their job is,

00:46:03.680 --> 00:46:08.070
then yes, it was absolutely the broader philosophical intent

00:46:08.070 --> 00:46:12.130
to make embarrassing behavior harder to commit.

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:16.210
But it will make local officials less likely to share information.

00:46:16.330 --> 00:46:20.870
Embarrassing behavior is just this side of abusive behavior.

00:46:22.470 --> 00:46:26.010
Only 12,000 out of a quarter million diplomatic cables

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:29.960
have been published so far, but they've already had...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:37.680
consequences. A dozen cables from Tunisia exposed widespread corruption there and helped

00:46:37.680 --> 00:46:41.920
fuel a revolution and, arguably, had a domino effect.

00:46:43.550 --> 00:46:47.830
I mean, I don't want to give WikiLeaks credit for the transformation of the Arab world,

00:46:47.970 --> 00:46:52.520
but, you know, to the extent that Tunisia influenced Egypt, these cables played some

00:46:52.520 --> 00:46:59.980
role in the overthrow of the Mubarak regime. And these things are having an...

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:03.990
that I don't think any of us imagined at the time

00:47:03.990 --> 00:47:07.420
when it was just somebody handing us a huge trove of secret documents.

00:47:08.720 --> 00:47:13.420
If you boil it down, look at what happened as a result of WikiLeaks.

00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:17.040
We gained a tremendous understanding of how government works,

00:47:17.120 --> 00:47:18.220
how wars are conducted.

00:47:19.790 --> 00:47:24.380
Balance the disclosures and the impact and the importance of the disclosures

00:47:24.730 --> 00:47:29.000
against everybody's fear of what was going to happen.

00:47:30.030 --> 00:47:31.630
Seems to me it ended up okay, right?

00:47:35.010 --> 00:47:37.910
Army Intelligence Private First Class Bradley Manning

00:47:37.910 --> 00:47:40.930
has been held for seven months in solitary confinement.

00:47:41.420 --> 00:47:45.200
He's in isolation as we keep our most serious criminals

00:47:45.200 --> 00:47:46.680
even though he has not been convicted.

00:47:46.900 --> 00:47:48.500
Free Bradley Manning!

00:47:49.860 --> 00:47:52.420
Manning has been charged with 22 counts,

00:47:52.600 --> 00:47:55.420
including unlawful possession of classified material

00:47:55.420 --> 00:47:56.840
and aiding the enemy.

00:47:58.750 --> 00:47:59.970
I was the Bradley Manning.

00:48:02.880 --> 00:48:06.100
Daniel Ellsberg, who famously leaked the Pentagon Papers

00:48:06.100 --> 00:48:09.380
hoping to end the Vietnam War, came to his defense.

00:48:09.790 --> 00:48:13.310
Bradley Manning is no more of a traitor than I am, and I'm not.

00:48:14.750 --> 00:48:17.570
And so did his computer science friends from Boston.

00:48:17.870 --> 00:48:19.570
I'm a friend of Bradley Manning's.

00:48:19.650 --> 00:48:21.950
I've been visiting him for the last few months in confinement.

00:48:22.760 --> 00:48:27.110
And I got to tell you, it's stuff like this that gives Bradley hope.

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:29.900
Manning has refused to cooperate with the government.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:34.520
investigation. Supporters say the government is trying to turn him.

00:48:35.360 --> 00:48:39.360
Bradley Manning has not been convicted of anything. He's not formally been indicted

00:48:39.360 --> 00:48:45.110
of anything. And he's being punished with this solitary confinement. I believe this

00:48:45.110 --> 00:48:50.040
is an effort to get Bradley Manning to crack under the pressure to maybe go for a plea deal

00:48:50.040 --> 00:48:54.330
or something. Just last month, Manning was moved from

00:48:54.330 --> 00:48:59.980
Quantico, Virginia to a military prison in Fort Leavenworth, Kansas. His lawyer said

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:01.720
his conditions have improved.

00:49:02.390 --> 00:49:06.150
Manning is expected to face his first pretrial hearing this summer.

00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:13.610
In England, Julian Assange is facing his own troubles.

00:49:14.870 --> 00:49:18.990
He is fighting extradition to Sweden, where he is accused of sex crimes,

00:49:19.430 --> 00:49:20.790
charges he denies.

00:49:22.670 --> 00:49:25.710
While the world's media wait outside court, inside,

00:49:26.330 --> 00:49:28.250
Julian Assange has been granted bail.

00:49:29.200 --> 00:49:29.980
Assange

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:36.810
now lives in the family home of a supporter three hours northeast of london he must report daily to

00:49:36.810 --> 00:49:44.790
the police a grand jury in the u.s is investigating his connection to the leaks you're tied up here

00:49:44.790 --> 00:49:50.340
in this house and you've offended a lot of people yeah but history is on our side when you expose

00:49:50.340 --> 00:49:54.820
powerful organizations they will there'll be ad hominem attacks there'll be all sorts of attacks

00:49:54.820 --> 00:49:59.220
and yes in our case they've been they've been in my personal case they've been rather hard

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:02.220
But it's not an unusual circumstance.

00:50:02.580 --> 00:50:04.000
What's next for WikiLeaks?

00:50:04.240 --> 00:50:08.470
WikiLeaks is continuing to step up its publishing speed.

00:50:08.950 --> 00:50:12.490
We are still involved in getting the majority of these cables out.

00:50:12.670 --> 00:50:16.110
And it does good. We can see the effects all around us.

00:50:17.310 --> 00:50:20.770
Last September, Daniel Domscheit-Berg left WikiLeaks

00:50:20.770 --> 00:50:25.010
to start his own whistleblowing website after a falling out with Assange.

00:50:26.570 --> 00:50:29.190
He doesn't care very much about your views.

00:50:30.120 --> 00:50:34.980
and it's either his way or the highway and it just really got abusive so to a point where

00:50:34.980 --> 00:50:40.720
if you criticized it he was starting he started to threaten people how do you feel about what

00:50:40.720 --> 00:50:48.320
you accomplished well i feel uh and that i think is the whole is the only important thing about

00:50:48.320 --> 00:50:55.500
wikileaks it has set in motion a cultural change in some way that it has created this whole debate

00:50:55.500 --> 00:50:59.920
that we are having today what is secrecy and is there any

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:04.780
for secrecy and what is the need for breaking these secrets and where do you have to draw

00:51:04.780 --> 00:51:06.100
the line in between these things.

00:51:06.430 --> 00:51:09.830
The goal is not to get rid of all secrets in this world.

00:51:10.350 --> 00:51:17.260
The goal is to foster transparency and that I think is a really important cause.

00:51:19.950 --> 00:51:24.710
Adrian Lamo, fearing for his safety, is now living in an undisclosed location.

00:51:24.770 --> 00:51:28.060
He works as a computer threat analyst.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:33.300
I couldn't even begin to speculate how it ends with me.

00:51:35.250 --> 00:51:39.750
If someone had told me that all this would happen,

00:51:40.940 --> 00:51:45.670
say, five years ago, I would have beggared belief.

00:51:47.330 --> 00:51:56.870
It's really living proof that truth is stranger than fiction.

00:52:05.690 --> 00:52:09.430
It was one year ago that Bradass87 reached out to Lamo.

00:52:10.700 --> 00:52:13.460
I wouldn't mind going to prison for the rest of my life.

00:52:15.370 --> 00:52:17.050
It's important that it gets out.

00:52:18.610 --> 00:52:22.570
I feel for some bizarre reason it might actually change something.

